# Liontrust Asset Management PLC
@@ Executive Remuneration Review, FY2016–FY2026 · LIO LN · Built on the audited FY2025 and FY2026 Remuneration Reports · Year-by-year analysis and UK asset management benchmarking · 31 August 2026

## Headline findings

| Metric | Position |
|---|---|
| **CEO single figure, FY2026** | **£667k** — of which **£nil** variable |
| **CEO single figure, FY2021 (peak)** | **£6,648k** — bonus and LTIP both at 100% of maximum |
| **Change, FY2021 to FY2026** | **-90.0%** |
| **Eleven-year cumulative CEO pay (FY2016–FY2026)** | **£32.15m** |
| **FY2026 executive bonus outcome** | **Nil for both directors** — scorecard scored 13.9%, then a new **share price underpin zeroed it** |
| **FY2026 LTIP vesting** | **Nil** — the FY2023 award lapsed in full. The FY2022 award vested at just **6.19%**, and only on the soft strategic component |
| **Base salary** | **£583,600 (CEO) / £445,600 (CFO)** — frozen for a third consecutive year |
| **Governing policy** | **2024 DRP**, approved Sept 2024 with **86.46%** support, effective 1 April 2025 |
| **Most recent remuneration vote** | **72.57% in favour** (27.43% against), 18 Sep 2025 — on **~32% turnout** |
| **CEO pay ratio (median)** | **5x**, down from **39x in FY2022** |
| **CEO shareholding vs 5x-salary requirement** | **450% — below guideline.** CFO at 598% is compliant |
| **Executive options outstanding after the Aug-2026 grant** | **~2.26m ~ 3.6% of issued capital** |

> **The verdict, restated on audited data.** Our 30 August draft understated how far this has already been fixed. Liontrust's executives were paid **nothing variable at all** in FY2026: no bonus, no LTIP vesting, and a third consecutive salary freeze. CEO pay is down 90% from its 2021 peak; the FY2023 LTIP lapsed in full; and a **share price underpin introduced this year overrode a 13.9% scorecard outcome and set the bonus to zero**. The Committee has also just rebuilt the FY2027 scorecard to cut Adjusted PBT from 55% to 20% and raise net flows and investment performance to 30% each — precisely the "stop paying for market beta" reform an activist would demand. **On implementation, this is now among the more disciplined remuneration committees in UK asset management.** Three things still deserve challenge, and one of them is new and serious: the **absolute TSR hurdle of 10%–15% over three years** (a maximum payout for ~4.8% a year), the **structural ratchet in share counts** created by granting percentage-of-salary awards at trough prices, and a **policy ceiling of 800% of salary** the Committee has declined to cap.

