## 1 · Basis of preparation, and corrections to our 30 August draft

**This report is built on primary source.** It restates our 30 August 2026 draft using the audited Directors' Remuneration Reports in Liontrust's Annual Report and Financial Statements **2026** (pages 107–134, signed 23 June 2026) and **2025** (pages 110–138, signed 24 June 2025), both by Miriam Greenwood OBE DL, Chair of the Remuneration Committee. Sections marked "(audited information)" in that report are audited by the Group's external auditor. Where we previously relied on secondary sources and analytics, several figures were materially wrong. We set them out in full below rather than quietly restating them.

### 1.1 What we got wrong

| Item | Our 30 Aug draft | **Audited actual** | Materiality |
|---|---|---|---|
| **CEO single figure FY2026** | ~£1.03m | **£667k** | **Major.** The £1.03m figure was FY2025, not FY2026 |
| **CEO single figure FY2024** | ~£1.0m | **£1,367k** | **Major** — understated by 37% |
| CEO single figure FY2023 | ~£2.0m | £1,933k | Minor |
| CEO single figure FY2021 | "£6.2m or £6.6m" | **£6,648k** | Resolved — the £6.2m figure was wrong |
| CFO single figure FY2026 | not obtainable | **£511k** | Now disclosed |
| **Governing policy** | "the 2022 policy remains in force" | **2024 DRP, approved with 86.46% support, effective 1 Apr 2025** | **Major.** A new policy was approved and we missed it |
| **LTIP structure pre-2025** | implied percentage-of-salary throughout | **Fixed number of shares with uncapped value at grant** until the 2024 DRP | **Major** — reverses part of our criticism |
| **10% vesting threshold** | "soft, against a 20–25% UK norm" | **The DRP permits up to 25%; the Committee voluntarily uses 10%** | **We had this backwards.** A lower threshold payout is *more* demanding |
| 2025 AGM disclosure undertaking | "has yet to be visibly delivered" | **Delivered** — update statement published 11 Feb 2026; LTI disclosure enhanced; bonus scorecard rebuilt | Correction |
| Board composition | Miriam Greenwood omitted | **Remuneration Committee Chair since Nov 2023** | Correction — also affects our equity note |
| Rebecca Shelley appointment | "2019" | **12 October 2021** | Correction |
| Compensation ratio | estimated 42–48% | **Total workforce remuneration -22%** (FY2026) and **-12.8%** (FY2025) | Now measured, not estimated |
| CEO single figure FY2025 | ~£1.0m | **£1,031k** | Confirmed |
| CFO single figure FY2024/FY2025 | not obtainable | **£937k / £727k** | Now disclosed |
| 2024 AGM remuneration report vote | not obtainable | **82.49% in favour** | The missing data point |

### 1.2 What we got right

Our derivations from the August 2026 LTIP grant RNS proved accurate to within rounding: we inferred a CFO salary of ~£446k (**actual £445,600**), an LTIP grant price of ~383p for the prior-year award (**actual 383.3p**), and prior-year grants of 452,962 and 247,038 shares (**actual 452,963 and 247,038**). The structural narrative — a profit-linked bonus pool replaced by a capped framework, followed by a collapse in variable pay — is confirmed in full.

> **A note on why this matters.** Our earlier draft graded the Committee "B- and improving". On audited data that grade was too harsh, and it was too harsh in a specific direction: we understated the severity of the pay outcomes the Committee has actually imposed, and we criticised two features (the vesting threshold and the LTIP share count) without knowing the policy history behind them. The revised assessment in §12 is materially more favourable — and the criticisms that survive are correspondingly sharper.

