## 2 · The remuneration architecture

### 2.1 Two policy regimes, and one structural change

| | **2022 DRP** (to 31 Mar 2025) | **2024 DRP** (from 1 Apr 2025) |
|---|---|---|
| Approved | GM 16 Feb 2022 — **54.1% in favour** | AGM 19 Sep 2024 — **86.46% in favour** |
| Annual bonus maximum | CEO 450% / CFO 350% of salary | **Unchanged** — CEO 450% / CFO 350% |
| **LTIP structure** | **Fixed number of shares — uncapped value at grant** | **Percentage of salary** — CEO max 350%, CFO max 250% |
| Pension | Workforce-aligned | **12.5%**, identical to the majority of the workforce |
| Shareholding requirement | — | **500% of salary**, plus a **two-year post-employment** requirement |
| Bonus deferral | Minimum 50% | Minimum 50%, into Liontrust shares or fund units |
| Malus / clawback | — | Malus from grant to vesting; **clawback for two years post-vesting**; applies to bonus and LTIP |
| Financial weighting in bonus | — | **At least 80%** must be financial |
| Entry-level bonus payout | — | Up to 25% of maximum, at Committee discretion |

**The single material change between the two policies was the LTIP.** The Committee's own description is precise: the award moved "from an award based on a fixed number of shares with an uncapped value at grant to a more traditional percentage of salary grant."

> **Why this matters more than it looks.** Under the old structure the CEO received **153,130 shares every year regardless of price**. As the shares fell, the value of each grant fell with them: £1,439k at 940p (2022), £1,152k at 752.5p (2023), £1,112k at 726p (2024). That was punitive in a downturn and, as the Committee concedes, uncapped in an upswing. The 2024 DRP replaced it with a conventional percentage-of-salary award — **which is what shareholders and proxy advisers asked for, and which passed with 86.46% support.** But at a depressed share price the same policy produces the opposite arithmetic: holding value constant requires far more shares. The FY2026 grant was **452,963 shares — a 196% increase in share count on the prior year** for a *higher* value of £1,736k. Neither structure is right. This is the central unresolved design problem in the policy, and §6.3 quantifies it.

### 2.2 The current elements of reward

| Element | CEO | CFO | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Base salary** | **£583,600** | **£445,600** | Frozen for FY2027. Future rises capped at the wider workforce average |
| **Pension / cash in lieu** | **12.5%** | 12.5% | Identical to the majority of the workforce — fully compliant with best practice |
| Benefits | Private medical, life, disability, travel insurance, EAP | Same | £6k and £5k respectively in FY2026 |
| **Annual bonus maximum** | **450% of salary** | **350% of salary** | 80%+ financial; balanced scorecard; **share price underpin** |
| Bonus deferral | Minimum 50%; **100% applied in FY2026** | Same | Into shares, or fund units once the shareholding requirement is met |
| **LTIP maximum** | **350% of salary** | **250% of salary** | Nil-cost options; 3-year performance; **2-year post-vest holding period** |
| LTIP threshold vesting | **10%** (policy permits up to 25%) | Same | Voluntarily set below the policy ceiling |
| **Total variable maximum** | **800% of salary (~£4.67m)** | **600% (~£2.67m)** | The Committee **considered and rejected** an overall cap on total remuneration |
| Shareholding requirement | **500% of salary**, 2 years post-employment | Same | CEO currently **below** at 450%; CFO compliant at 598% |
| All-employee plans | SIP (£1,800 matched **2:1**), SAYE (£500/month, up to 20% discount), CSOP | Same | Satisfied by market-purchased shares — no dilution |

