## 4 · The FY2026 annual bonus: a nil outcome, and how it got there

### 4.1 The scorecard (audited)

| Metric | Weight | Threshold | Target | Max | **Actual** | **Weighted result** |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Financial — 80%** | | | | | | |
| Adjusted PBT (incl. performance fees) | **55.0%** | 85.0% | 100.0% | 107.5% | **84.0%** | **0.0%** |
| Distribution effectiveness (net flows) | 12.5% | 67.0% | 100.0% | 116.7% | **(178.9%)** | **0.0%** |
| Investment performance (AuMA in 1st/2nd quartile, 1Y and 3Y) | 12.5% | 50.0% | 67.5% | 80.0% | **33%** | **0.0%** |
| **Non-financial — 20%** | | | | | | |
| Enhance client experience and outcomes | 5.0% | n/a | n/a | n/a | 80% | 4.0% |
| Diversify product range and investment offering | 5.0% | n/a | n/a | n/a | 67% | 3.4% |
| Broaden distribution and client base | 5.0% | n/a | n/a | n/a | 50% | 2.5% |
| Strengthen technology, data and digital capability | 5.0% | n/a | n/a | n/a | 80% | 4.0% |
| **Total scorecard** | **100%** | | | | | **13.9%** |
| **Share price underpin applied** | | | | | | ** to  NIL** |

**The financial half failed comprehensively.** The Adjusted PBT target was set at **£37m** and the outcome was 84% of it. Net flows were **-£4,184m against a target of -£1,500m**, producing a distribution effectiveness score of **minus 178.9%**. Blended investment performance was **33% against a 50% threshold**. All three financial measures — 80% of the scorecard — scored zero.

### 4.1a The prior year for comparison: the FY2025 scorecard

| Metric | Weight | Threshold | Target | Max | **Actual** | **Weighted result** |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Financial — 70%** | | | | | | |
| Change in Adjusted PBT (target £60.6m) | **50.0%** | 80.0% | 100.0% | 110.0% | **80%** | **5.0%** |
| Distribution effectiveness — net flows vs a **budget of +£70m inflows** | 10.0% | 90% | 100% | 110% | **(7,006%)** | **0.0%** |
| Investment performance (1Y/3Y/5Y weighted) | 10.0% | 67.5% | 75.0% | 82.5% | **34%** | **0.0%** |
| **Non-financial — 30%** | | | | | | |
| Enhance the client experience and outcomes | 7.5% | n/a | n/a | n/a | **0%** | 0.0% |
| Increase and diversify the product range | 7.5% | n/a | n/a | n/a | 25% | 1.9% |
| Expand distribution and the client base | 7.5% | n/a | n/a | n/a | **0%** | 0.0% |
| Technology, data and digital capability | 7.5% | n/a | n/a | n/a | 80% | 6.0% |
| **Total scorecard** | **100%** | | | | | **12.9%** |

> **The FY2025 net-flow score deserves to be read twice.** The Committee budgeted for **net inflows of £70m** and the business delivered **net outflows of £4,904m** — producing a scored outcome of **minus 7,006%**. Setting a positive-flow budget in the fourth consecutive year of heavy redemptions was, with hindsight, not a credible target. The FY2026 target was reset to **net outflows of £1,500m**, which is a considerably more honest basis — and was still missed, at £4,184m.
>
> **Two of the four FY2025 non-financial objectives scored zero**, including client experience and distribution expansion, and the Committee declined to apply any upward moderation. This is not a scorecard being marked generously.

### 4.2 The share price underpin: the most important governance development of the year

Following the 27.43% vote against the 2025 remuneration report, the Committee consulted shareholders, published an update statement on **11 February 2026**, and introduced a **share price underpin**:

> If total shareholder return over the financial year is less than **37.1p** — 10% of the 371p share price at 31 March 2025 — the annual bonus scorecard outcome is **set to nil, regardless of the actual scorecard outcome.**

TSR for FY2026 was negative. The shares fell from **371p to 241p, a decline of 35%.** The underpin therefore overrode the 13.9% scorecard and produced a **nil bonus for both executives**.

> **This is the single best feature of Liontrust's remuneration framework and it deserves unambiguous credit.** It is a hard, mechanical, non-discretionary link between executive variable pay and the shareholder's actual experience — precisely what the 2021–22 profit-pool structure lacked. It was introduced in direct response to shareholder feedback, and **in its first year of operation it bit.** Very few UK asset managers have anything equivalent.
>
> **Two caveats.** The Committee states the underpin "will be in place for next year, **but may not be in future years**" — it is not a permanent feature of the policy and could be withdrawn without a shareholder vote. And the FY2027 hurdle resets to **24.1p**, being 10% of the *new, lower* 241p base. A falling share price mechanically lowers the bar each year. **Shareholders should press for the underpin to be made permanent and for the hurdle to be anchored, not reset annually.**

### 4.3 Deferral

Both executives deferred **100% of their variable remuneration** in FY2026 — against a policy minimum of 50%. The DBVAP history shows this is a genuine escalation, not a one-off:

| Award in respect of | John Ions — % of bonus deferred | Value | Vinay Abrol — % deferred | Value |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| FY2022 | 69% | £1,915,000 | 50% | £786,000 |
| FY2023 | 50% | £310,000 | 50% | £184,000 |
| FY2024 | 50% | £263,000 | 50% | £156,000 |
| **FY2025** | **100%** | **£339,000** | **100%** | **£201,000** |
| **FY2026** | **n/a — nil bonus** | **£nil** | **n/a — nil bonus** | **£nil** |

~ DBVAP awards are nil-price options over Liontrust shares and/or units in Liontrust-managed funds, exercisable one third each year over three years. No further performance conditions apply, the Committee having satisfied itself on performance when determining the original bonus.

