## 5 · The LTIP: grants, lapses, and whether the in-flight awards can pay

### 5.1 Grant history (audited)

| Award (FY) | Granted | **Shares — CEO** | Share price used | **Face value — CEO** | Shares — CFO | Face value — CFO | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| FY2022 | 23 Jun 2021 | 53,389 | 1,630.0p | £870k | 35,182 | £573k | **Vested at just 6.19%** — 50,085 and 33,005 options forfeit |
| FY2023 | 23 Jun 2022 | 153,130 | 940.0p | £1,439k | 112,295 | £1,056k | **LAPSED IN FULL, Jun 2025** |
| FY2024 | 22 Jun 2023 | 153,130 | 752.5p | £1,152k | 112,295 | £845k | In flight — tested Jun 2026 |
| FY2025 | 27 Jun 2024 | 153,130 | 726.0p | £1,112k *(190% of salary)* | 112,295 | £815k *(183%)* | In flight — tests Jun 2027 |
| **FY2026** | **26 Jun 2025** | **452,963** | **383.3p** | **£1,736k** | **247,038** | **£947k** | In flight — tests Jun 2028 |
| **FY2027** | **6 Aug 2026** | **566,203** | ~292.8p *(derived)* | ~£1,657k *(derived)* | **308,797** | ~£905k *(derived)* | Granted post year-end |

~ Under the pre-2025 fixed-share structure the percentage of salary was simply whatever the share price made it — the FY2025 grant landed at **190%** of the CEO's salary. The FY2026 award, the first under the 2024 DRP, was **297.5%** of salary (85% of the 350% maximum) and 212.5% for the CFO — so the move to a percentage-of-salary framework was a step up in *percentage* terms as well as share count. FY2027 award: 284% and 203% respectively, capped by the Committee at a 25% increase in share numbers. Grant price for FY2027 derived from the disclosed share counts and percentages against audited salaries.

### 5.2 The FY2023 award lapsed in full — the test that mattered

| Condition | Weight | Entry | Target | Stretch | **Actual** | Vested |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Adjusted Diluted EPS | 60% | 8.5% | 11% | 16.75% | **(19)%** | **Nil** |
| Relative TSR vs FTSE 250 | 40% | Median | — | Top quintile | **Bottom quartile** | **Nil** |

A £1,439k award to the CEO and £1,056k to the CFO **paid nothing.** The plan worked exactly as intended.

#### The FY2022 award vested at 6.19% — and only on the soft component

| Condition | Weight | **Actual** | Vested |
|---|---|---|---|
| Absolute TSR (from 1,559.5p on 23 Jun 2021) | 20% | **-14%** against a 10% p.a. threshold | **Nil** |
| Relative TSR vs FTSE All-Share TR | 20% | **-15%** | **Nil** |
| Adjusted diluted EPS ex-performance fees growth | 30% | **+0.61% p.a.** (73.46p to 74.84p) against a 15% p.a. target | **Nil** |
| Strategic objectives — net inflows | 15% | Net outflows against an inflow target | **Nil** |
| Strategic objectives — investment performance | 7.5% | 36.11% of funds in the top two quartiles | **Nil** |
| Strategic objectives — four qualitative sub-measures | 7.5% | Judgement-based | **6.19%** |

> **Every financial and quantitative measure scored zero. The only payout came from the qualitative sub-component** — talent retention, product range, client coverage and risk controls, assessed by the Committee. The sums were small (3,304 shares to the CEO, worth £29,421 on vesting against £53,855 on grant), but the design point matters: **when a plan's hard measures all fail, a soft 7.5% residual should arguably fail with them.** The 2024 DRP removed the strategic-objectives component from the LTIP entirely, replacing it with investment performance. That was the right fix.

### 5.3 Can the in-flight awards pay? The FY2026 award examined

The FY2026 award (452,963 shares to the CEO, tested June 2028) carries three conditions:

| Condition | Weight | Threshold (10% payout) | Maximum (100% payout) | **Where the business actually is** |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Adjusted diluted EPS (ex-performance fees) | **30%** | **41.9p** | **51.3p** (target 46.6p at 50%) | **FY2026 actual: 35.66p ex-performance fees.** Threshold requires **+17.5%**; maximum requires **+44%** |
| **Absolute TSR** (from 26 Jun 2025) | **40%** | **10% total** | **15% total** | From a 383.3p grant price against ~306.5p today — **currently negative** |
| Investment performance — weighted AuMA in top two IA quartiles, 3Y and 5Y | **30%** | 50.0% | 75.0% | **FY2026 blended 1Y/3Y measure was 33%** — below even the bonus threshold |

> **The finding that should concern shareholders most is the absolute TSR hurdle.** A **15% total shareholder return over three years pays 100% of a 40% weighting.** That is approximately **4.8% a year** — below the long-run return of the FTSE 250, below most estimates of the cost of equity, and below what an investor could have earned in gilts over much of the performance period. The relative FTSE 250 underpin lets the Committee flex the outcome by only **up to 10% of the award**, which mitigates but does not fix it.
>
> **And the hurdle resets downward with the share price.** The FY2026 award measures TSR from 383.3p. The FY2027 award, granted in August 2026, measures from approximately **292.8p** — so the same 15% now requires only ~337p rather than ~441p. **Granting percentage-of-salary awards at a depressed price delivers a double benefit: more shares, and a lower absolute return hurdle on each of them.** This is the sharpest remaining criticism in this report and it is not addressed anywhere in the policy.

**In fairness:** measured from their actual grant prices, the FY2024 and FY2025 awards (752.5p and 726p) are deeply underwater and appear very likely to lapse, as the FY2023 award did. The EPS condition on the FY2026 award is also demanding on current trajectory. The criticism is of the hurdle *design*, not of a windfall that has been paid.

### 5.4 Outstanding options and dilution

| At 31 March 2026 | Performance-conditioned | Not performance-conditioned | **Total** |
|---|---|---|---|
| John Ions | 759,223 | 93,477 | **852,700** |
| Vinay Abrol | 471,628 | 58,950 | **530,578** |
| **Combined** | **1,230,851** | **152,427** | **1,383,278** |
| *Plus the FY2027 grant (6 Aug 2026)* | *875,000* | *—* | *875,000* |
| **Combined, post-August 2026** | **2,105,851** | **152,427** | **~2,258,278** |
| **As % of ~62.97m shares in issue** | **3.34%** | 0.24% | **~3.59%** |

**Context that our earlier draft lacked.** The Company operates a formal policy that **dilution from employee share awards will not exceed 10% in any rolling ten-year period**, uses an employee benefit trust to manage it, and satisfies SIP and CSOP awards with **market-purchased shares**, which carry no dilutive effect. The 3.6% figure therefore sits inside a disclosed and monitored cap.

**The point that survives.** The FY2026 and FY2027 LTIP grants together total **1,575,001 shares — 2.50% of issued capital**. The £10m buyback completed in June 2026 retired 3,764,532 shares. **Two years of executive LTIP grants equal roughly 42% of the entire buyback programme.** Returning capital through buybacks while issuing equity to executives at trough prices is a real economic tension, whether or not it breaches a dilution cap.

