## 6 · Pay for performance

### 6.1 Executives, workforce and shareholders compared

| Percentage change vs prior year | FY2022 | FY2023 | FY2024 | FY2025 | **FY2026** |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **CEO salary** | 0% | **+58%** | +6% | 0% | **0%** |
| **CEO bonus** | 0% | -78% | -15% | -36% | **-100%** |
| **CFO salary** | 0% | **+28%** | +6% | 0% | **0%** |
| **CFO bonus** | 0% | -77% | -15% | -36% | **-100%** |
| **Staff salary (average)** | +12% | +11% | +6% | +5% | **+4%** |
| **Staff bonus (average)** | +103% | -38% | -7% | -21% | **-24%** |

> **The FY2026 column is the answer to the question shareholders have been asking for four years.** Executive bonuses fell **100%**. Staff bonuses fell **24%**. Executive salaries were frozen; staff salaries rose 4%. The Committee differentiated **against** the executives, decisively. That is the opposite of the 2021–22 pattern and it should be recognised as such.

### 6.2 Relative importance of spend on pay, FY2025 to FY2026 (audited)

| Measure | **FY2025 to FY2026** | FY2024 to FY2025 |
|---|---|---|
| Adjusted profit before tax, excluding performance fee profit | **-38%** | -25.9% |
| Adjusted profit before tax | **-37%** | -28.4% |
| **Total member and employee remuneration** | **-22%** | **-12.8%** |
| **Dividend spend** | **-75%** | **-0.3%** |

**The two-year comparison is the more revealing one.** In FY2025, adjusted profit fell 28.4% and workforce pay fell 12.8% — while **dividend spend fell just 0.3%**, because the board was still defending the 72p dividend out of capital. Shareholders bore almost nothing that year and everything the next. In FY2026 the pattern reversed: **dividend spend -75%, workforce pay -22%**, against gross profit -22% — so the compensation-to-gross-profit ratio was held flat, which is competent cost management. Pay still falls more slowly than profit, which is normal in asset management where people are the product, but the FY2025 column is the one an activist will cite: **a year in which the workforce and the executives absorbed a cut and the distribution policy did not.**

### 6.3 The CEO pay ratio (voluntary disclosure)

Liontrust's UK headcount is below the 250-employee threshold, so the CEO pay ratio requirement **does not apply**. The Company discloses it anyway, using Option A — full individual pay data for all UK staff. That is a credit to the Committee.

| | FY2022 | FY2023 | FY2024 | FY2025 | **FY2026** |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Lower quartile ratio | **69x** | 21x | 15x | 12x | **8x** |
| **Median ratio** | **39x** | **13x** | **10x** | **9x** | **5x** |
| Upper quartile ratio | **16x** | 7x | 5x | 5x | **3x** |

| FY2026 reference data | Lower quartile | Median | Upper quartile |
|---|---|---|---|
| Workforce single figure | £81k | **£129k** | £216k |
| Workforce salary component | £70k | £100k | £140k |
| **CEO single figure** | | **£667k** | |

**The trajectory is extraordinary: a median ratio of 39x in FY2022 has fallen to 5x.** A ratio of 5x is remarkably low for a UK listed company — FTSE 350 medians typically run 30x–60x, and UK asset managers commonly sit in the 15x–40x range. The FY2022 figure of 39x, by contrast, was squarely in the normal range, which is a useful corrective: the old structure was not an outlier on the *ratio*, it was an outlier on **how the money was generated** — a formula that paid 100% six years running. It reflects both a genuinely well-paid workforce (median single figure of £129k) and a CEO taking nothing variable.

