## 7 · Shareholding and alignment

### 7.1 Against the 5x-salary requirement (audited, at 31 March 2026)

| | Ordinary shares held | Unvested (non-performance) | **Value** | **% of salary** | vs 500% requirement |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **John Ions — 31 Mar 2026** | 1,041,256 | 85,013 | **£2,623k** | **450%** | **Below** |
| John Ions — 31 Mar 2025 | 1,035,620 | – | £3,842k | **658%** | Compliant |
| **Vinay Abrol — 31 Mar 2026** | 1,074,380 | 50,846 | **£2,659k** | **598%** | **Compliant** |
| Vinay Abrol — 31 Mar 2025 | 1,068,744 | – | £3,965k | **891%** | Compliant |

**Three observations.**
1. **The CFO holds more shares than the CEO**, both absolutely and relative to salary — an unusual and telling inversion after 16 and 30 years respectively.
2. **The CEO fell below the guideline during FY2026, not before it.** At 31 March 2025 he stood at **658%**; a year later, at **450%**. He *bought* shares over the period — the holding rose from 1,035,620 to 1,041,256. **The entire shortfall is the 35% fall in the share price from 371p to 241p.** That is an important distinction: this is not a director selling down, and the guideline is doing what a share-price-denominated test does in a drawdown. It remains a live compliance point on which the Committee should state a remediation timetable.
3. A **two-year post-employment shareholding requirement** applies — good practice, and directly relevant to any change-of-control scenario.

### 7.2 Non-executive shareholdings

| Director | Ordinary shares held |
|---|---|
| Luke Savage (Chair) | 15,000 |
| Miriam Greenwood (Remuneration Committee Chair) | 2,750 |
| Rebecca Shelley (SID) | 2,082 |
| Mandy Donald (Audit & Risk Chair) | 1,579 |

**Non-executive alignment is minimal.** Three of the four hold fewer than 3,000 shares — under £10,000 at the current price. The policy merely "encourages" NEDs to use a percentage of fees to buy shares. Given a board that may have to adjudicate an offer for the company, this is thin.

### 7.3 The post-year-end share dealing

On **29 April 2026** John Ions sold 89,849 shares and purchased 76,298; Paige Ions, a person closely associated with him, sold 215,041 and purchased 43,258; and his dependent children purchased 185,334. The Company discloses that **his net shareholding including persons closely associated with him remained unchanged.** On 5 May 2026 both executives purchased 2,043 SIP shares each.

**Assessment:** on the disclosed facts this is an intra-family transfer, most plausibly estate or tax planning, and is net-neutral in economic exposure. It is properly disclosed. We flag it because a 215,041-share disposal by a PCA is the kind of transaction that attracts attention out of context, and shareholders may reasonably ask the Company to confirm the rationale.

### 7.4 The alignment ratio versus peers

| | CEO annual pay | CEO shareholding value | **Pay as % of shareholding** |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Liontrust — John Ions** | **£667k** | **£2,623k** | **25.4%** |
| Ashmore — Mark Coombs (founder) | ~£229k | ~£331m | **0.07%** |

> Mark Coombs owns 32.19% of Ashmore and is, economically, a pure owner: his annual pay is worth 0.07% of his stake, so essentially all of his incentive comes from the share price. John Ions' annual pay is worth **a quarter of his entire shareholding** — meaning a 25% rise in the Liontrust share price delivers him roughly what one year's pay does. **The gap has narrowed sharply from our earlier estimate of 38%, because FY2026 pay was so much lower than we assumed.** The structural point nonetheless stands: for the incumbent executives the recurring compensation stream remains a materially larger economic interest than the control premium on their equity, and that shapes the board's incentives on any approach for the company.

