## 8 · The FY2027 framework: what the Committee has changed

### 8.1 The bonus scorecard has been rebuilt

| Metric | **FY2026 weighting** | **FY2027 weighting** | Change |
|---|---|---|---|
| Adjusted profit before tax | **55.0%** | **20%** | **-35 points** |
| Distribution effectiveness (net flows) | 12.5% | **30%** | **+17.5 points** |
| Investment performance | 12.5% | **30%** | **+17.5 points** |
| *Financial subtotal* | *80%* | *80%* | *unchanged* |
| Strategic objectives | 20% (four measures) | **10%** | |
| **River Global integration** | — | **10%** | **New** |
| Share price underpin | Introduced | **Retained** (TSR < 24.1p  to  nil) | |

> **This is a substantive, shareholder-friendly redesign and the Committee deserves credit for it.** Cutting Adjusted PBT from 55% to 20% directly addresses the criticism that has dogged Liontrust since 2021 — that executives were paid for a profit number driven by market beta, and one that excludes the amortisation and impairment of the very acquisitions they chose to make. Raising **net flows and investment performance to 30% each** ties pay to the two variables that actually determine whether this franchise recovers, and which management genuinely controls. Adding a **specific 10% weighting for River Global integration** creates accountability for the acquisition at the point of execution rather than at the point of announcement.
>
> **One residual concern:** Adjusted PBT still carries 20%, and it remains an Alternative Performance Measure whose definition is set by management, subject to Audit & Risk Committee approval. In FY2024 adjusted PBT of £67.4m sat against a **statutory loss before tax of £0.6m**. Any weight on this measure should be accompanied by a statutory profit sense-check.

### 8.2 Fixed pay

Base salary is **frozen for a third consecutive year** at £583,600 and £445,600. The wider workforce receives 3% on average, "focused on specific cases where roles and responsibilities have changed". Non-executive fees are also frozen. Future executive salary rises are capped at the wider workforce average. Pension remains at 12.5%, identical to the majority of the workforce.

### 8.3 The FY2027 LTIP

At the date of the report the Committee was **still considering the appropriate level of award "to take into account the current share price"** — an explicit acknowledgement of the ratchet problem identified in §5.3. The grant, made on 6 August 2026, was **capped at a 25% increase in share numbers** and landed at 284% and 203% of salary, below the 350%/250% policy maxima. The performance conditions are absolute TSR with a relative FTSE 250 underpin (40%), adjusted diluted EPS ex-performance fees from a **starting point of 35.66p** (30%), and investment performance over three and five years (30%).

