## 10 · Shareholder voting record

| Meeting | Resolution | Votes for | % for | Votes against | **% against** | Withheld |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **GM, 16 Feb 2022** | Directors' Remuneration Policy | — | **54.10%** | — | **45.90%** | — |
| **GM, 16 Feb 2022** | Long Term Incentive Plan | — | **55.60%** | — | **44.40%** | — |
| **AGM, 22 Sep 2022** | Directors' Remuneration Report | — | **53.50%** | — | **46.50%** | — |
| AGM, 21 Sep 2023 | Directors' Remuneration Report | Passed | n/d | | n/d | |
| **AGM, 19 Sep 2024** | Directors' Remuneration Report | **24,567,967** | **82.49%** | **5,215,593** | **17.51%** | 2,417,556 |
| **AGM, 19 Sep 2024** | **Directors' Remuneration Policy (2024 DRP)** | **25,746,170** | **86.46%** | **4,032,396** | **13.54%** | 2,416,697 |
| **AGM, 18 Sep 2025** | Directors' Remuneration Report | **14,619,673** | **72.57%** | **5,525,591** | **27.43%** | 1,555,403 |
| **AGM, 17 Sep 2026** | Directors' Remuneration Report | **Pending** | | | | |

~ **A disclosure inconsistency worth noting.** The FY2025 Annual Report states the votes for the 2024 Remuneration Policy as **25,746,170**; the FY2026 Annual Report states **24,746,170**. Both disclose 86.46%, and 86.46% of (x + 4,032,396) resolves to 25,746,170 — so the FY2026 report carries a typographical error in the vote count. The percentages are unaffected. The 2023 percentages remain unobtainable from the sources available.

### 10.1 Two findings from the vote data

**First, 2024 broke the pattern — then 2025 partially reversed it.** After 45.9% and 46.5% against in 2022, the 2024 AGM passed the new **policy** with **86.46%** support and the **remuneration report** with **82.49%** — the first clean sweep since 2021. Dissent then rose again to **27.43%** at the 2025 AGM. Because the policy itself had just been endorsed, that vote was a verdict on **implementation and disclosure**, not on the framework — which is exactly what the Committee's consultation identified, and what the 11 February 2026 update statement and the FY2027 scorecard redesign address.

**Second — and this has not been widely noticed — turnout is remarkably low.** Total votes cast at the 2025 AGM were **20,145,264**, against roughly 63.8m shares in issue: a turnout of about **32%**.

> **The implication for the September 2026 AGM.** GAM Holding AG's special situations funds hold **3,155,000 shares**. At 2025's turnout that single holding would represent **approximately 15.7% of all votes cast** — before any coordination with other institutions. On a register where the top 20 hold only ~50% and turnout runs at a third, **a 5% holder is not a minority voice at this meeting; it is a bloc capable of pushing a remuneration resolution below 50%.** Any shareholder intending to vote should assume the outcome is genuinely contestable.

## 11 · Peer benchmarking

### 11.1 Structure

| Practice | **Liontrust** | UK asset management norm | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| Bonus funded from a profit pool | **Removed in 2022** | Common at partnerships; rare and poorly regarded at listed companies | **Fixed** |
| **Share price underpin on the bonus** | **Yes — and it produced a nil outcome in FY2026** | **Very rare** | **Materially better than peers** |
| Annual bonus maximum | **450% of salary (CEO)** | 150–250% typical for a listed mid-cap | **Outlier — top of range** |
| LTIP maximum | **350% of salary (CEO)** | 150–250% typical | **Outlier — top of range** |
| Total variable maximum | **800% of salary**; no overall cap on total remuneration | 300–500% typical | **Significant outlier** |
| **Actual variable paid, FY2026** | **Nil** | Widely still paid | **Far better than peers** |
| Bonus deferral | Minimum 50%; **100% applied** | 40–60% typical | **Better than peers** |
| LTIP metrics | TSR (40%), adjusted EPS (30%), **investment performance (30%)** | TSR and EPS standard; investment performance used by the better-governed | **Better than peers** |
| **Absolute TSR hurdle** | **10% threshold / 15% maximum over three years** | Relative TSR vs an index is standard; absolute hurdles usually far higher | **Materially weaker than peers** |
| LTIP threshold vesting | **10%**, though the policy permits 25% | 20–25% is the UK norm | **Better than peers** — we corrected this |
| Post-vest holding | **Two years** | Two years is best practice | **In line** |
| Pension | **12.5%, workforce-aligned** | Alignment now standard | **In line — fully compliant** |
| Shareholding requirement | **500% of salary**, 2 years post-employment | 200–300% typical | **Well above peers** |
| Malus and clawback | Malus to vesting; **clawback 2 years** | Standard | **In line** |
| CEO pay ratio | **Disclosed voluntarily** despite exemption | Often omitted when exempt | **Better than peers** |
| Combined CEO/CFO tenure | 16 years and 30+ years | Unusually long | **A structural governance issue** |

### 11.2 Quantum

| Company | CEO | **Latest total CEO pay** | AUM | Market cap | **Pay per £bn AUM** | **Pay as % of market cap** | Fixed / variable mix |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Liontrust** | **John Ions** | **£667k** | **£24.3bn** | **~£193m** | **~£27k** | **~0.35%** | **100% / 0%** |
| Premier Miton | Mike O'Shea | ~£923k | ~£9.0bn | ~£58m | ~£103k | ~1.59% | 38% / 62% |
| Jupiter | Matthew Beesley | ~£2.16m | ~£68.4bn | ~£0.8bn | ~£32k | ~0.27% | 22% / 78% |
| Schroders | Richard Oldfield | ~£1.81m | ~£770bn | ~£9.9bn | ~£2.4k | ~0.018% | 21% / 79% |
| Aberdeen Group | Jason Windsor | ~£3.19m | ~£500bn+ | ~£3.5bn | ~£6.4k | ~0.09% | 24% / 76% |
| Ashmore | Mark Coombs *(founder)* | ~£229k | ~£40bn | ~£1.47bn | ~£5.7k | ~0.016% | 44% / 56% |

~ Liontrust figures are audited FY2026. Peer figures are indicative, drawn from public compensation disclosures and analytics as at August 2026, and should be refreshed against a live data source before use in a voting decision.

**On restated data, Liontrust screens well.** At £667k the CEO is paid less than Premier Miton's — a manager a third of Liontrust's size — and roughly a third of Jupiter's. Pay per £bn of AUM falls from our earlier estimate of £42k to **£27k, now below Jupiter's**. Pay as a percentage of market capitalisation falls from 0.53% to **0.35%**. And with **zero variable pay**, Liontrust's CEO has by some distance the most conservative pay mix in the peer group, against 76–79% variable at the three large listed managers.

**The quantum criticism has therefore largely dissolved.** What remains is a criticism of **policy ceilings and hurdle calibration**, not of what is being paid.

[PAGEBREAK]

## 12 · Assessment

### 12.1 Green flags

1. **Nil variable pay in FY2026** — no bonus, no LTIP vesting, for either executive.
2. **The share price underpin** — a hard, mechanical link to shareholder experience that overrode a 13.9% scorecard in its first year of operation.
3. **The FY2027 scorecard redesign** — Adjusted PBT cut from 55% to 20%; net flows and investment performance raised to 30% each; a dedicated 10% weighting on River Global integration.
4. **The FY2023 LTIP lapsed in full**, and the FY2024/FY2025 awards look likely to follow.
5. **Salary frozen for a third year**, with future rises contractually capped at the workforce average.
6. **100% bonus deferral**, against a 50% policy minimum.
7. **A 500%-of-salary shareholding requirement** with a two-year post-employment tail — well above UK norms.
8. **Threshold vesting voluntarily set at 10%** against a policy permitting 25%.
9. **Pension at 12.5%, identical to the workforce.**
10. **CEO pay ratio disclosed voluntarily** despite exemption; median down from 13x to 5x.
11. **Executives differentiated against**: staff bonuses -24%, executive bonuses -100%.
12. **Genuine responsiveness to the 2025 vote** — consultation, an update statement on 11 February 2026, and concrete changes delivered.

### 12.2 Red flags

1. **The absolute TSR hurdle is far too low.** 15% total return over three years — roughly **4.8% a year** — pays 100% of a 40% weighting. Below the cost of equity, below the FTSE 250's long-run return, and mitigated only by a relative underpin that can flex the outcome by at most 10% of the award. **This is the most serious remaining defect.**
2. **The TSR base resets downward each year.** The FY2026 award measures from 383.3p; the FY2027 award from ~292.8p. A falling share price makes each successive hurdle easier in cash terms while simultaneously increasing the share count. **There is no disclosed windfall-adjustment mechanism.**
3. **The policy ceiling of 800% of salary** — a theoretical CEO package of ~£5.3m, about 2.7% of market capitalisation — remains in force, and the Committee **considered an overall cap on total remuneration and decided against it**.
4. **The share price underpin is not permanent.** The Committee states it "may not be in future years", and the FY2027 hurdle has already reset from 37.1p to 24.1p.
5. **The CEO is below the 500% shareholding requirement** at 450%, with no disclosed remediation timetable.
6. **Adjusted PBT remains a bonus metric at 20%** — an Alternative Performance Measure defined by management that showed £67.4m against a statutory loss of £0.6m in FY2024.
7. **The 2023 salary increases (+58% CEO, +28% CFO) are permanent** and compound through every variable maximum, which is expressed as a percentage of salary.
8. **Non-executive shareholdings are minimal** — three of four directors hold under 3,000 shares — on a board that may have to adjudicate an offer.
9. **Extreme executive tenure**: 16 years and 30+ years, with no disclosed succession planning in the remuneration context.

### 12.3 Revised grade

> **B+ on implementation; C on policy design.** Our 30 August draft graded this **B-** on incomplete information; the audited record does not support that. The Committee has imposed a nil variable outcome, introduced a binding share price underpin, rebuilt the scorecard around flows and fund performance, frozen pay for three years and let a £2.5m LTIP lapse in full. **On what it has actually done, this is now among the more disciplined remuneration committees in UK listed asset management.**
>
> **The framework it operates within is another matter.** An 800%-of-salary ceiling with no total cap, and a 15% three-year TSR hurdle for maximum vesting, are not consistent with the restraint the Committee is currently exercising. **The correct shareholder posture is to support the 2026 remuneration report and press hard on the TSR hurdle and the policy ceiling at the next policy renewal.**

