## 14 · Twelve questions for the Remuneration Committee Chair

*Several of our earlier questions have now been answered by the audited report and are dropped. These are the ones that remain.*

1. The absolute TSR condition pays **100% of a 40% weighting for a 15% total return over three years** — approximately 4.8% a year, below most estimates of the cost of equity. What is the rationale, and will the Committee raise it?
2. The TSR measurement base fell from 383.3p to approximately 292.8p between the FY2026 and FY2027 grants. What prevents a windfall from a beta-driven recovery, given that a lower base makes the same percentage hurdle easier in cash terms while the share count simultaneously rises?
3. The share price underpin produced a nil bonus in its first year and was described as something that "may not be in future years". **Will the Committee commit to making it a permanent feature of the next policy**, and to anchoring the hurdle rather than resetting it to 10% of each year's opening price?
4. The Committee considered an overall cap on total remuneration and decided against it. Given a policy ceiling of 800% of salary at a company with a £193m market capitalisation, what would change that view?
5. The CEO's shareholding is **450% of salary against a 500% requirement**. What is the remediation timetable, and does the Committee regard the shortfall as material given the two-year post-employment requirement?
6. Adjusted profit before tax retains a 20% bonus weighting. In FY2024 it showed £67.4m against a **statutory loss before tax of £0.6m**. Will the Committee introduce a statutory profit sense-check?
7. The FY2026 LTIP requires adjusted diluted EPS ex-performance fees of **41.9p at threshold against 35.66p actual**. Does the Committee regard the in-flight awards as realistically capable of vesting, and if not, what is their retention value?
8. Base salary rose 58% and 28% in 2023 and has been frozen since. Every variable maximum is expressed as a percentage of that salary. Has the Committee considered rebasing?
9. Non-executive directors hold between 1,579 and 15,000 shares. Will the Committee introduce a **mandatory** NED shareholding requirement, given the board may have to adjudicate an offer for the company?
10. On 29 April 2026 the CEO and persons closely associated with him executed offsetting transactions in over 400,000 shares, described as leaving the net holding unchanged. Will the Company confirm the rationale?
11. The FY2027 scorecard introduces a 10% weighting for River Global integration. What are the specific, measurable integration criteria, and will the outcome be disclosed against them?
12. With the CEO at 16 years and the CFO at 30+, how does the Committee weigh the retention rationale for large LTIP grants against the succession risk of extreme tenure?

[PAGEBREAK]

## 15 · Conclusion

On audited data, the story of Liontrust's executive remuneration is one of a framework that failed badly and has since been comprehensively repaired.

From FY2017 to FY2021 the annual bonus paid at **100% of maximum in five consecutive years** and the LTIP at 100% in three, producing a peak CEO single figure of **£6,648k**. That was not performance-linked pay; it was a profit-share funded directly from Adjusted Profit Before Tax, and profit tracked markets. The 2022 policy reset severed the link and hard-capped the bonus. The 2024 DRP, approved with **86.46%** support, replaced an uncapped fixed-share LTIP with a conventional percentage-of-salary award.

The outcomes since have been severe and, in FY2026, absolute. CEO pay fell to **£667k**, of which **nothing** was variable. The scorecard scored 13.9% and a newly introduced **share price underpin set it to nil**. The FY2023 LTIP lapsed in full. Salary has been frozen for three years. The median CEO pay ratio has fallen from 13x to **5x**. Staff bonuses fell 24% while executive bonuses fell 100%. And the FY2027 scorecard has been rebuilt to cut Adjusted PBT from 55% to 20% and put **net flows and investment performance at 30% each**.

> **The honest conclusion is that we were too hard on this Committee, and for the wrong reasons.** What remains is narrower but sharper. A maximum LTIP payout for **15% total shareholder return over three years** is not a stretch target — it is roughly 4.8% a year, and the measurement base resets lower with every depressed grant. A policy ceiling of **800% of salary**, with an overall cap explicitly considered and rejected, is inconsistent with the restraint the Committee is actually exercising. And a share price underpin that has just proved its worth is, on the Committee's own words, not guaranteed to survive.
>
> **Support the FY2026 remuneration report. Press the TSR hurdle, the permanence of the underpin, and the policy ceiling.** On a register where turnout runs at a third and a 5% holder commands roughly a sixth of the votes cast, that pressure is more likely to succeed than at any point in the last four years.

## 16 · Sources

**Primary.** Liontrust Asset Management PLC, *Annual Report and Financial Statements 2026*, Remuneration Report, pages 107–134, signed by Miriam Greenwood OBE DL, Chair of the Remuneration Committee, 23 June 2026. Sections identified as "(audited information)" include the single total figure of remuneration, pensions, LTIP awards, historic LTIP awards, DBVAP awards, SIP shares, directors' shareholdings, shareholding requirement, compensation for loss of office and payments to former directors. Filed at Companies House under company number 02954692.

**Supporting company disclosure.** Director/PDMR Shareholding RNS of 6 August 2026 (FY2027 LTIP grants); the shareholder update statement of 11 February 2026; Result of AGM announcements for 22 September 2022, 21 September 2023, 19 September 2024 and 18 September 2025; the General Meeting result of 16 February 2022; and TR-1 holdings notifications of July and August 2026.

**Third-party.** Citywire, Portfolio Adviser, City A.M., LexisNexis Research & Legal Analysis, Investment Week, Money Marketing and Kalkine Media for the 2021–22 policy episode; and published compensation and ownership analytics for Jupiter Fund Management, Ashmore Group, Premier Miton, Schroders and Aberdeen Group.

~ **Caveats.** All Liontrust figures in sections 3 to 10 are taken directly from the audited Remuneration Report. Three items are analyst derivations, marked as such: the FY2027 LTIP grant price of ~292.8p and its implied face values, calculated from the disclosed share counts and percentages of audited salary; the ~32% AGM turnout, calculated from disclosed votes against an approximate share count; and the peer alignment ratios in §7.4 and §11.2. Peer remuneration, AUM and market capitalisation figures are indicative and drawn from secondary sources. The 2023 AGM remuneration vote percentage was not obtainable and is marked n/d. Where this report differs from our 30 August 2026 draft, the audited figure governs; §1.1 lists every correction.

